export abstract anchor FactoryIntegration:
    deliveryTargets:
        A target record carries an identifier, kind, destination, baseline and
        current revision, state, required checks, receipt, lease, and fencing
        epoch. The delivery unit is the order-target pair. A target may be
        explicitly none, recorded with its authorizer at planning time.
        Required targets are frozen when the order is planned; changing them
        needs an engineer-approved order revision.
    deliveryDestination:
        The order's prompt determines where completed work is delivered, such
        as an integrated branch, a pull request, or the main branch. Asking for
        a delivery default during onboarding is the preferred direction; the
        exact default and fallback behavior remain unresolved.
    integrationCandidates:
        Each target has one integration queue and one target lease with a
        fencing epoch. A candidate is assembled and checked before completion
        assessment. Candidates are ordered by dependency, then order priority,
        then readiness time.
        The integrator re-applies the candidate onto the current target revision
        together with its recorded prerequisite revisions, runs the target's
        required checks on the combined result, and records the exact candidate
        revision. A revision mismatch re-applies and rechecks. Assessment binds
        to that exact revision; if the candidate must change after assessment,
        it is reassessed before delivery.
        Git targets update by compare-and-swap on the target revision. Non-git
        targets use an adapter with intent, outcome, and read-back; unchanged
        means the read-back matches the baseline. A merger resolution creates a
        new candidate revision, and every affected order's assessor revalidates
        it. If assessors disagree on the same integrated revision, the target
        enters a blocked split state until an authorized decision resolves it.
    targetLeaseLifecycle:
        A target lease records target, holder, epoch, acquisition, expiry,
        state, and the queue head it covers. It is durable ownership, not a lock
        held across an external call. Acquisition is atomic with queue-head
        selection. A multi-target order acquires and releases one target lease
        per target effect in sorted target order; it never holds several target
        leases at once. No worktree or capacity lease is held while acquiring a
        target.
        Renewal uses a heartbeat and TTL. Long checks renew or release and
        reacquire. Check results and assessments are epoch-bound; if the epoch
        advances, the result is discarded and rerun. An in-doubt delivery holds
        the lease and epoch and is never re-fenced while undisposed. Its
        disposition deadline is a separate clock from the TTL: at expiry the
        target stays blocked, the lease stays held, and escalation continues
        until an engineer disposition or an authorized forced disposition.
        The epoch gates dispatch, intent commit, and receipt binding. Git
        targets use revision compare-and-swap. A non-git target declares its
        version token, where the token is enforced, whether read returns it,
        where the idempotency key is recorded, and the exact proof of applied
        or not-applied. A read-back-only target that cannot prove the outcome is
        unresolved. A multi-object mismatch is partial and holds the lease until
        compensation or disposition.
    leaseConformance:
        Cells pair a positive control with a fault: acquire, renew, and release;
        a live stale holder with the same lease identity and older epoch
        attempting publication after expiry is rejected while a current-epoch
        control succeeds; renewal extends validity past the original expiry;
        crash after acquisition, during checks, and before any effect recovers
        at expiry with zero effects; non-git CAS unsupported yields unresolved
        while a supported-CAS control succeeds; disjoint resources with
        overlapping intervals run concurrently; intersecting resources
        serialize; a mid-run key addition releases and reacquires without
        deadlock; both sorted acquisition orders are exercised; an orphan
        worktree stays isolated; a fenced writer cannot corrupt the preservation
        snapshot; and cleanup releases after preservation. Each cell records
        lease identity, state, epoch, key set, target revision, CAS token or
        read-back outcome, receipt binding, effect counts, and release order. A
        cell that never reaches its condition is not passed.
    deliveryOutcomes:
        Outcomes are pending, running, succeeded, partial, failed, unresolved,
        or unnecessary. The idempotency key is the order, target, and canonical
        integrated revision. A receipt proves one attempt at that revision and
        records the target revision, external identifier, effect count, checks,
        and actor.
        Retries read back the target and never repeat a confirmed effect. An
        in-doubt effect becomes unresolved and holds the target delivery lease
        and epoch until disposition: applied adopts or reconstructs the receipt,
        not-applied releases and retries, unknown requires engineer-authorized
        replacement.
    partialDelivery:
        Partial delivery is nonterminal. It records the remainder. An
        engineer-approved scope reduction creates a follow-up order in a
        non-running state, acquires the target lease and epoch, and then
        activates. The original order's target retries are suppressed by the
        same revision. A crash between steps leaves the follow-up inert and the
        original claim held. After an approved reduction the removed target is
        no longer required, so the original order may complete without it.
        Required-target failure before reduction never completes the order.
    compensation:
        A target or effect declares whether it is reversible and names its
        compensator. The declaration is approved above the implementer; an agent
        cannot declare its own effect reversible. Compensation runs only after
        the original effect is resolved, holds the target lease, is effect-keyed,
        idempotent, and reverse-ordered across multiple targets. Its outcomes are
        succeeded, failed, partial, or unresolved. Partial compensation becomes
        unresolved. Compensation records whether it invalidates the receipt and
        whether a successor order is required.
    deliveryRoles:
        A delivery owner coordinates targets, receipts, and retries. A merger
        resolves integration conflicts. An integrator assembles combined
        candidates and runs combined checks. An independent assessor accepts the
        result and is distinct from every required implementer, the delivery
        owner, the merger, and the integrator.
    finalAcceptance:
        Final acceptance is a recorded operation with an actor, assessor
        identity, checked revision, and receipt set. It requires successful
        receipts for every required target, passing combined checks, and the
        completion assessment. The assessor may refuse acceptance; a refused
        acceptance after a successful external effect blocks the order and
        requires compensation or a successor order. The top-tier implementer
        case uses the engineer or a fresh independent reviewer with recorded
        authority; if neither exists, acceptance stays blocked.
    deliveryMatrix:
        Conformance cells are typed by target kind, fault, and outcome, with a
        mandatory setup and injection, a path-reached assertion, an expected
        pre- and post-target state, exact effect counts inside a quiescence
        window, and a receipt check. Cells cover an unmerged prerequisite
        integrated once; two concurrent candidates with a conflict and a
        reassessed revision; combined checks failing with the target unchanged;
        a target unavailable then succeeding; partial delivery with an approved
        reduction and follow-up; a duplicate retry with exactly one effect; an
        in-doubt delivery resolved by lookup; reversible compensation and an
        irreversible refusal; cleanup after receipts; and an explicitly none
        target. A cell that never reaches its path is not passed.
